WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:04.830
So traveling, traveling is basically the concept

2
00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.230
of either moving from server to server

3
00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.750
or connecting to a server in the first place,

4
00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.080
or indeed our server moving to a new map.

5
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.519
So two notions that are instantly worth kind of highlighting

6
00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:21.640
is the idea of seamless and non seamless travel.

7
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.559
These are kind of slightly more meaningful

8
00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:25.039
from the client's perspective.

9
00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:28.839
Seamless travel means that there will be a non-blocking,

10
00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:34.200
i.e. asynchronous operation when traveling to the new server.

11
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.560
On the other hand, in contrast, non seamless travel

12
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.079
is gonna cause a blocking load.

13
00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:42.039
So this is a little bit like when you load a texture

14
00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:44.960
or a sound, if you do a synchronous or a blocking load,

15
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.719
it's basically going to,

16
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.920
a blocking call is gonna force,

17
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.600
disconnect the client from the server.

18
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.840
And then once that's done,

19
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.840
it will reconnect the client to the same server,

20
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.409
which will now have the new map loaded.

21
00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:03.210
So there are three scenarios in which non seamless,

22
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:04.969
i.e. the kind of blocking load

23
00:01:04.969 --> 00:01:07.569
where they disconnect and reconnect must occur.

24
00:01:07.569 --> 00:01:10.489
When loading a map for the first time,

25
00:01:10.489 --> 00:01:12.930
either we don't have this map kind of cached,

26
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.650
when connecting to a new server for the first time,

27
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.390
and when ending a multiplayer game and starting a new one.

28
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.510
And then there are kind of three core functions

29
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:25.150
that drive and utilize these different forms of travel.

30
00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.670
So we'll kind of quickly look at those.

31
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.349
So first we have the browse function.

32
00:01:30.349 --> 00:01:33.430
Essentially, this is a hard reset when loading a new map,

33
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.950
and it will always result in non seamless travel,

34
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.189
as we kind of said about the disconnecting, reconnecting.

35
00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:41.430
This causes the server to disconnect all current clients

36
00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.790
before traveling to the new map.

37
00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:48.790
And the dedicated servers can't travel to other servers.

38
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:54.629
So when we're talking about traveling,

39
00:01:54.629 --> 00:01:56.670
we can travel via a URL.

40
00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.430
That URL can either be a server IP,

41
00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.510
or it can be a kind of a local map content location.

42
00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.269
This has to be a map location, a server.

43
00:02:06.269 --> 00:02:07.349
In the context of server,

44
00:02:07.349 --> 00:02:08.870
it cannot travel to another server

45
00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.949
because servers can't connect to servers.

46
00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:14.300
So it has to be a local map URL.

47
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.900
And then we have server travel and client travel.

48
00:02:18.139 --> 00:02:23.139
So server travel is held in UWorld,

49
00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:25.539
client travel is held inside the player controller,

50
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.500
and this kind of occurs in order.

51
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.900
So server travel is for the server only,

52
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.020
and it will jump the server to a new level.

53
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.460
All connected clients are gonna follow that server,

54
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.500
and the server is in charge of calling that function.

55
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.460
And it's the server itself that will then call

56
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.419
client travel to the connected clients,

57
00:02:44.419 --> 00:02:46.219
to each of the clients that are connected.

58
00:02:46.219 --> 00:02:48.460
So at that point, client travel would get called,

59
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:53.180
and well, okay, the server can call client travel.

60
00:02:53.219 --> 00:02:56.620
If client travel is called from a client,

61
00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.979
then it's about traveling to a new server.

62
00:02:58.979 --> 00:03:00.620
If client is called from the server,

63
00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:01.900
then it's generally gonna be an instruction

64
00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.539
to transition to a new map.

65
00:03:03.539 --> 00:03:05.419
And this is gonna be that seamless travel

66
00:03:05.419 --> 00:03:07.939
where we're going to maintain that connection to the server

67
00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:09.620
and just move to a new map.

68
00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.900
But obviously we already talked about the various situations

69
00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.139
where this is impossible,

70
00:03:13.139 --> 00:03:15.300
i.e. we don't have this map loaded before,

71
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.300
we've never connected to the server before,

72
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:23.280
or we're ending a multiplayer game.

73
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.159
So the other kind of thing that's worth mentioning

74
00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:26.680
when we're talking about traveling

75
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.560
or transitioning between servers, or more importantly, maps,

76
00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.039
it's worth bearing in mind that the game mode

77
00:03:34.039 --> 00:03:37.719
is one of these things that will persist by default,

78
00:03:37.719 --> 00:03:40.159
but also the game mode has this function,

79
00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:43.159
getSeamlessLevel, a travel actors list,

80
00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:45.280
that basically gives you the ability to specify

81
00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.120
a list of actors that will travel

82
00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.240
between levels seamlessly.

83
00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.360
So anything that you need to kind of persist

84
00:03:52.759 --> 00:03:54.479
to kind of keep your game up and running,

85
00:03:54.479 --> 00:03:56.520
the things that you only expected to get spawned once,

86
00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.479
that might be things that you put

87
00:03:58.479 --> 00:04:00.719
in this SeamlessTravel actor list.

88
00:04:02.539 --> 00:04:05.219
All player control, all controllers for players

89
00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:08.340
with valid player states as part of the,

90
00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:13.340
players from the server's perspective will persist.

91
00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:16.459
All player controllers from the server's perspective

92
00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:17.500
will persist.

93
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.139
And all local player controllers,

94
00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:24.220
so if you're the connected client,

95
00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.100
your player controller will persist between travel.
